    {
      "title": "Rupee falls 5 paise to 94.98 against US dollar during early trade",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/finance/news/rupee-falls-5-paise-to-94-98-against-us-dollar-during-early-trade-126102600204_1.html",
      "description": "The rupee traded in a narrow range and fell 5 paise to 94.98 against the US dollar in early trade on Monday, weighed down by elevated crude oil prices and geopolitical tensions in the Middle East.\nForex traders said the rupee opened on a negative note on risk aversion in global markets amid escalation of tensions in the Middle East. However, softness in the US Dollar and US treasury yields cushioned the downside.\nAt the interbank foreign exchange market, the rupee opened at 94.92, then lost ground to touch 94.98, lower by 5 paise over its previous close.\nOn Friday, the rupee depreciated for the second consecutive day and fell 12 paise to close at 94.93 against the US dollar.\nThe Indian rupee opened slightly lower on elevated crude oil prices and geopolitical tensions in the Middle East. However, softness in the US Dollar and US treasury yields cushioned the downside,\" said Anuj Choudhary, Research Analyst, Mirae Asset Sharekhan.\n\"We expect the rupee to trade with a slight negative",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-26T04:45:07"
